    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm studying for sec+ and i'm a bit stuck. i get what a hash function is, but why do we actually need to add a salt to the password before hashing it? like, if the hash is already unique, doesn't that do the job? thanks",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A hash function, while it creates a unique-looking string, is *not* enough on its own for passwords. If you don't salt, an attacker can use pre-computed tables, like rainbow tables, to quickly crack hashes. A salt is a unique, random string added to the password *before* it gets hashed, which makes every password hash unique, even if two users have the same password. This defeats the efficiency of rainbow table attacks.",
