    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so the salt is basically just extra noise to mess with the pre-computed tables. what happens if the salt is too short though? like if it's only a couple of characters, does that actually help much or is it still pretty easy to brute force?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "If the salt is too short, it diminishes its effectiveness, making the defense weaker, though it doesn't completely negate the security. A very short salt is much easier for an attacker to manage when trying to build their own rainbow tables or perform dictionary attacks against a subset of users. It's like throwing a tiny pebble into a massive river—it might cause a ripple, but it won't stop the current. For robust security, the salt needs to be sufficiently long and truly random, often 16 bytes or more, depending on the standard you are following.",
